TITLE:: NdefRackSpeakers
summary:: the speaker setups the rack's panners pan among
categories:: Libraries>NdefRack
related:: Classes/NdefRack, Classes/NdefRackLib, Classes/VBAP

DESCRIPTION::
A table of named speaker setups - stereo, quad, 5.1, a ring of eight, a cube, a
dome - and, for each, what a panning module needs: how many channels it has,
where every speaker stands, and the numbers that put a sound between them. The
library's code::vbap:: and code::ambi:: modules are made on one
(link::Classes/NdefRack#*make::, link::Classes/NdefRack#*setSpeakers::), and a
module of your own can be (the code::speakers:: key of a module file, see
link::Classes/NdefRackLib::).

It needs no window and no server: everything is worked out in the language.

code::
NdefRackSpeakers.names;                // the setups, those that come with the rack first
NdefRackSpeakers(\octo).dirs;          // [[-22.5, 0], [22.5, 0], [67.5, 0], ...]
NdefRackSpeakers('5.1').numChannels;   // 6: five speakers and the sub
NdefRackSpeakers.add(\studio, [-30, 30, -90, 90, -150, 150]);
NdefRackSpeakers.remove(\studio);
::

subsection:: Directions

A speaker is code::[azimuth, elevation]:: in degrees, seen from the middle of
the room. Azimuth 0 is straight ahead and strong::positive is to the right:: -
30 is the right speaker of a stereo pair, -110 the left surround - with 180
behind. Elevation 0 is ear height, positive is up, 90 overhead. The directions
are in the order of the output channels.

A sub has no direction and is not among them: code::lfe:: is the index of its
output channel, 3 in 5.1 (L R C LFE Ls Rs). The panners send it nothing but what
their code::sub:: control says.

subsection:: The setups

table::
## strong::name:: || strong::label:: || strong::channels:: || strong::the speakers, in channel order::
## code::\stereo:: || stereo || 2 || L R, 30 degrees either side
## code::\quad:: || quad || 4 || L R Ls Rs: front pair, back pair, at 45 and 135
## code::\quadring:: || quad ring || 4 || the same four, clockwise from front left
## code::'5.0':: || 5.0 || 5 || L R C Ls Rs: 30, 0, 110
## code::'5.1':: || 5.1 || 6 || L R C LFE Ls Rs
## code::'7.1':: || 7.1 || 8 || L R C LFE Lss Rss Lrs Rrs: sides at 90, rears at 135
## code::'5.1.4':: || 5.1.4 || 10 || 5.1, then four above, 30 up, over L R Ls Rs
## code::'7.1.4':: || 7.1.4 || 12 || 7.1, then four above, 30 up, at 45 and 135
## code::\hex:: || hexagon || 6 || a ring, clockwise from front left, the front between two speakers
## code::\octo:: || octo || 8 || a ring, clockwise from front left, the front between two speakers
## code::\octopairs:: || octo pairs || 8 || the same eight as left right pairs, front to back
## code::\octofront:: || octo centre || 8 || a ring, clockwise from a speaker straight ahead
## code::\ring16:: || ring 16 || 16 || a ring, clockwise from front left
## code::\cube:: || cube || 8 || the lower four, then the upper four, each as quad
## code::\dome13:: || dome 8+4+1 || 13 || octo on the floor, four at 45 up, one overhead
## code::\dome24:: || dome 12+8+4 || 24 || twelve on the floor, eight at 30 up, four at 60 up
::

These cannot be replaced or removed. The surround setups follow ITU-R BS.775 and
BS.2051; the rings and domes are regular shapes, not any one room's numbering -
add your room under a name of its own.

subsection:: A setup never changes

Adding a name again with other speakers makes a new setup object and tells the
dependants - code::NdefRackSpeakers.changed(\setups, name):: - and so does
removing one. link::Classes/NdefRack:: listens: every module on that name moves
to the new speakers. The old object stays right for whatever was built on it.

subsection:: Two ways to pan

link::#-ambiGains:: is ambisonic panning with core UGens: the direction is
written in harmonics - circular on a ring, spherical when speakers have height -
weighted by focus and decoded by the matrix with orthonormal columns nearest to
the harmonics sampled at the speakers. The squared gains sum to 1 in every
direction, at every focus, on every setup (measured, and equal to VBAP's level).
The link::#-order:: is chosen once per setup: the highest, 3 at most, with no
more harmonics than speakers, whose energy vector at focus 0.5 points within 45
degrees of where it is told. A square has order 1, a hexagon 2, an octagon 3;
5.1 only 1 - at 2 its rear points the wrong way.

link::#-vbapData:: is what sc3-plugins' link::Classes/VBAP:: reads from its
buffer. VBAP is right inside the speakers and wrong outside them - past the ends
of a stereo pair the far speaker comes out with its phase turned over, below the
floor of a dome the sound lands on whatever triangle is least wrong - so
link::#-vbapAngles:: keeps a direction inside. VBAP reads its buffer once, when
the synth starts, and a buffer that is not there then stops the server: hand it
the data in a code::LocalBuf::, as the code::vbap:: module does.

code::
(
var setup = NdefRackSpeakers(\octo);
setup.ambiGains(22.5, 0, 0.5).round(0.01).postln;    // most on the second speaker
setup.ambiGains(22.5, 0, 0.5).squared.sum.postln;    // 1.0
setup.vbapGains(0, 0).round(0.01).postln;            // the front pair, 0.71 each
)
::


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
The setup of that name. The same as link::#*at::.
ARGUMENT:: name
a link::Classes/Symbol::.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers::, or nil.

METHOD:: at
ARGUMENT:: name
a link::Classes/Symbol::.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers::, or nil for a name the table does not have.

METHOD:: for
A name, a setup or nothing, as a setup.
ARGUMENT:: setup
a name, an link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers::, or nil for the link::#*default::.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers::, or nil for a name the table does not have.

METHOD:: default
RETURNS:: code::\stereo:: - what a panner is made on when no setup is named.

METHOD:: names
RETURNS:: every setup's name: those that come with the rack, in the table's order, then yours in the order you added them.

METHOD:: userNames
RETURNS:: the names of the setups you added.

METHOD:: add
Add a setup of your own, or replace one. Warns and answers nil for a name that
comes with the rack, for fewer than 2 or more than 55 speakers, and for
directions that are not numbers. Adding exactly what is there does nothing.
code::
NdefRackSpeakers.add(\studio, [-30, 30, -90, 90, -150, 150]);
NdefRackSpeakers.add('my room', [[-45, 0], [45, 0], [0, 0], [-135, 0], [135, 0], [0, 70]], 3, "room 5.1+1");
NdefRackSpeakers.remove(\studio); NdefRackSpeakers.remove('my room');
::
ARGUMENT:: name
a link::Classes/Symbol::.
ARGUMENT:: dirs
the speakers in channel order: code::[azimuth, elevation]:: in degrees each, or a
plain number for an azimuth at ear height. The sub is not among them.
ARGUMENT:: lfe
the output channel of a sub, counted from 0, or nil.
ARGUMENT:: label
what the window's drop-down says; the name if not given.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers::, or nil.

METHOD:: remove
Take a setup of your own out of the table. A module on it stays on the speakers
it had.
ARGUMENT:: name
a link::Classes/Symbol::.
RETURNS:: the setup removed, or nil.

METHOD:: maxOrder
The highest order link::#-ambiGains:: works at, 3 at most. Read when a setup's
order is first asked for.

METHOD:: maxError
How many degrees off a setup's energy vector may point before a lower order is
taken: 45.


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: name
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Symbol::.

METHOD:: dirs
RETURNS:: the speakers, code::[azimuth, elevation]:: in degrees each, in channel order, without the sub.

METHOD:: lfe
RETURNS:: the output channel of the sub, counted from 0, or nil.

METHOD:: label
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::, for a menu.

METHOD:: builtin
RETURNS:: true for a setup that comes with the rack.

METHOD:: numSpeakers
RETURNS:: how many speakers, the sub not counted.

METHOD:: numChannels
RETURNS:: how many output channels: the speakers, and one for a sub.

METHOD:: isCurrent
RETURNS:: whether the table still holds this setup under its name - false once it was replaced or removed.

METHOD:: sameAs
ARGUMENT:: otherDirs
directions, as link::#*add:: takes them.
ARGUMENT:: otherLfe
a channel or nil.
RETURNS:: whether those are this setup's speakers, in this order, with this sub.

METHOD:: mode
RETURNS:: code::\pair:: for two speakers - the sound moves along the line between
them; code::\ring:: for speakers all at one height; code::\sphere:: for anything
with height.

METHOD:: order
RETURNS:: the ambisonic order link::#-ambiGains:: works at on this setup, 1 to 3. Worked out once.

METHOD:: decoder
RETURNS:: the decoding matrix at that order, a row per speaker. Worked out once.

METHOD:: ambiGains
A gain per speaker for a direction, by ambisonics. Numbers in, numbers out;
control rate signals in, control rate signals out - core UGens only. The squares
sum to 1. On a ring the elevation only spreads the sound over the speakers; on a
pair the rear folds onto the front, and hard right is azimuth 90.
ARGUMENT:: azi
degrees, positive to the right.
ARGUMENT:: ele
degrees, positive up.
ARGUMENT:: focus
0 in-phase - every speaker in step, the widest; 0.5 max-rE; 1 basic - the
sharpest, the speakers opposite out of phase.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array::, one gain per speaker.

METHOD:: measure
How well a direction comes out of link::#-ambiGains::. Numbers only.
ARGUMENT:: azi
degrees.
ARGUMENT:: ele
degrees.
ARGUMENT:: focus
0 to 1.
ARGUMENT:: atOrder
an order of your own, for the question; the setup's if not given.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event:: - code::(power:, amp:, re:, minG:, err:)::, the
sum of the squares, the sum, the length of the energy vector, the lowest gain,
and how many degrees the energy vector is off.

METHOD:: measureGains
The same for gains from anywhere.
ARGUMENT:: gains
one per speaker.
ARGUMENT:: azi
where they were aimed, degrees.
ARGUMENT:: ele
degrees.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event::, as link::#-measure::.

METHOD:: vbapData
What link::Classes/VBAP:: wants in its buffer for this setup
(code::VBAPSpeakerArray:getSetsAndMatrices::), worked out once. On an arc of
three speakers or more, the pair VBAPSpeakerArray makes of its two ends is left
out - it would take the whole front.
RETURNS:: an array of numbers, or nil without sc3-plugins and for speakers VBAP
cannot work with: two opposite each other, speakers with height all in one plane
through the listener.

METHOD:: aziRange
RETURNS:: code::[from, to]:: in degrees when the speakers are an arc VBAP must stay
inside - a ring with a gap wider than 170 degrees; nil when they go all the way round.

METHOD:: eleRange
RETURNS:: code::[lowest, highest]:: elevation VBAP can be asked for on speakers with
height - the lowest speaker when no triangle of speakers holds straight down,
-90 when one does, and the same upward; nil on a ring or a pair, where VBAP does
not read the elevation.

METHOD:: vbapAngles
A direction as VBAP can take it on this setup, kept inside link::#-aziRange:: and
link::#-eleRange::. Numbers or control rate signals.
ARGUMENT:: azi
degrees.
ARGUMENT:: ele
degrees.
RETURNS:: code::[azi, ele]::.

METHOD:: vbapGains
What VBAP makes of a direction at no spread, worked out in the language from
link::#-vbapData::. Numbers only - for checks.
ARGUMENT:: azi
degrees.
ARGUMENT:: ele
degrees.
RETURNS:: one gain per speaker, or nil without data.

METHOD:: vbapCovers
ARGUMENT:: azi
degrees.
ARGUMENT:: ele
degrees.
RETURNS:: whether a pair or a triangle of speakers lies around that direction.

METHOD:: withLfe
The speakers' channels with the sub's put in at its place.
ARGUMENT:: channels
one per speaker.
ARGUMENT:: sub
the sub's channel.
RETURNS:: an array of link::#-numChannels:: channels - the channels as they are when the setup has no sub.

METHOD:: data
Everything code::vbap:: and code::ambi:: need, as plain numbers - what their
code::speakers:: makers are given, so the functions they make, and the code the
rack exports for them, need no NdefRackSpeakers.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event:: - code::(name:, dirs:, numSpeakers:, lfe:, mode:, order:, decoder:, exact:, weights:, axis:, vbapData:, aziRange:, eleRange:)::.

METHOD:: addCode
RETURNS:: the line that adds this setup, as the rack's code export writes it for a setup of your own.
